    "explanation": "To both parties. The platform's own GPS trace for this trip (EV-GPS-00509, COMPLETE, weight 1.0) records 17.14 km against a platform optimal of 9.6 km taken from the trip record. That is an excess of 7.54 km, or 78.5% of optimal. It passes both halves of the POL-RD-01 test (greater than 20% of optimal and greater than 0.8 km absolute), so a route deviation is ESTABLISHED, and it is a MATERIAL deviation. The governing figure is the trace distance, not the trip record's rounded 17.1 km copy. The trace is not impaired for policy purposes under POL-RD-07: 193 fixes at a 10-second cadence, 100% coverage, an empty gaps array, max_gap_sec 0.0 and no jump over 500 m, so no independent corroboration is required and the POL-RD-10(3) escalation is not engaged.\n\nNo excuse displaces the deviation. POL-RD-04 (rider-requested detour) needs a rider instruction in the chat log, and the chat log for this trip holds no messages at all, so it cannot run. POL-RD-05 (relocation) needs an amended endpoint and a stored amended optimal, and pickup_amended_at, dropoff_amended_at, original_pickup, original_dropoff and amended_optimal_distance_km are all null, so the booked 9.6 km optimal stands. POL-RD-03 (traffic exoneration) needs a traffic advisory naming a road the optimal route uses; the only advisory artifact, EV-ADVIS-00509-01, is MISSING with weight_multiplier 0.0 and issue code MISSING_ARTIFACT, so there is nothing to test the driver's explanation against. The driver's own account ('navigation kept re-routing... I got lost') names no advisory evidence ID, so under POL-RD-13(1) an assertion that cites no artifact cannot be invoked against one: the absent advisory is a gap in the bundle, not a POL-RD-10(5) contradiction. I therefore reject the driver's request to hold the disputed amount; his traffic explanation carries no weight under POL-RD-08, and the deviation stands at the measured figure. No POL-RD-10 trigger fires: TRP-00509 matches across the trip record, trace and fare breakdown, no timestamps disagree, the refund is well below both the SGD 150.00 ceiling and charged_total, and every charged line is accounted for.\n\nFare. The fare model is METERED and quoted_fare_sgd is null, so POL-RD-11 does not apply and POL-RD-02 governs. recomputed_fare = (3.80 base fare + 0.75 x 9.6 km + 0.28 x 14.0 min) x 1.0 surge multiplier read from the fare breakdown + 0.80 platform fee + 0 ERP + 0 surcharge + 0 waiting fee - 0 promo discount = SGD 15.72. charged_total is SGD 26.39, so the refund is 26.39 - 15.72 = SGD 10.67, rounded UP to SGD 10.70. That is 40.4% of the amount charged, above the SGD 0.50 de minimis and below the 90% share that would make it a FULL_REFUND, so the outcome is a PARTIAL_REFUND. Nothing is added on top: there is no ERP, toll, airport surcharge or waiting-time fee to pass through, and POL-RD-09's anti-double-count rule bars stacking any pass-through figure on the SGD 10.70.\n\nOn the rider's consequential losses (the missed bus, the replacement ticket and the lost evening): POL-RD-02 makes the remedy arithmetic and traceable to lines of the fare breakdown only, so those losses are not compensable here. On the driver's point that no FULL_REFUND uplift or conduct referral follows: the POL-RD-06 pattern test is run and fails, because deviation_flags_90d is 0 against 150 completed trips in the window, below both thresholds, so neither the uplift nor the POL-RD-06(c) referral fires. No driver compensation arises either: the billed distance of 17.1 km is only 0.04 km short of the 17.14 km recorded on the trace, far below the 1.0 km shortfall POL-RD-04 requires.\n\nOutcome: SGD 10.70 is returned to the rider; the balance of the SGD 26.39 charged stands.",
